An array of pointers is not a pointer, and a name cannot tell them apart.

WHAT IT IS. `char *a[2]; p = a[1]; p[0]` segfaults. The identical program with
`long *` in place of `char *` passes, and the emitted code says why:

    char* a[2]; p=a[1]            long* a[2]; p=a[1]
      mov_x0,1                      mov_x0,1
                                    mov_x14,8 / mul_x0,x14,x0     <- stride
      add_x0,x1,x0                  add_x0,x1,x0
      ldrsb_x0,[x0]                 ldr_x0,[x0]                   <- width

No stride, and one SIGNED BYTE of an eight-byte pointer.

THE CAUSE IS A LINE THE FILE ALREADY APOLOGISES FOR. postfix_expr_array carries
a long comment saying "A name comparison cannot answer a question about width.
The element size can, and it is the same expression the stride below already
uses, so the two now agree by construction instead of by coincidence." Directly
underneath it:

    if(match("char*", current_target->name))
    {
        assign = load_value(1, TRUE);
    }
    else
    {
        emit_mul_register_zero_with_immediate(element_size, ...);
    }

The comparison was never removed. It decides BOTH the load width and whether to
scale, and it is right for `char *p; p[i]` -- element is a char, stride 1, load
1 -- and wrong for `char *arr[8]` -- element is a char POINTER, stride 8, load
8. current_target is `char *` in both cases. That is precisely why a name
cannot separate them, and the comment above says so.

element_size already separates them six lines up, through indexing_an_array.

HOW IT WAS FOUND, AND WHY THE SUITE COULD NOT.

MICRO-C.md names this blind spot in its own words:

    "Every array-of-pointers probe used `long*`, where the element width and
    the pointed-at width are both 8 -- so no case could detect a wrong choice
    between them."

It then says case 31 closed it. Case 31 made the two widths differ for a STRUCT
element. It never did for a pointer-to-char element, so the suite has been
green over a live bug every round since.

What found it was borrowing stage 2's conformance corpus -- 426 small C
programs written for pico-c, for a different rung, by someone not looking for
this compiler's bugs. NINE of them fail on this one line: rows 394, 396, 397,
398, 400, 401, 402, 403 and 404. A suite written FROM bugs already found
measures what has been fixed; a corpus written for something else measures what
remains.

WHY IT MATTERS FOR tcc. `char *include_stack[32]`, `char **argv`, and about
forty more declarations across the translation unit. This is the first bug in
several rounds that is squarely on tcc's own data structures.

MEASURED

    difftest      55 -> 56 pass, both architectures, nothing regressed
    corpus        410 -> 419 of 426 on aarch64
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures
    tcc           compiles, links and runs as before; the fault has NOT moved

diff --git a/cc_core.c b/cc_core.c
index df65944..d8f20ec 100644
--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -2222,11 +2222,31 @@ void postfix_expr_array(void)
 		assign = load_value(element_size, current_target->is_signed);
 	}
 
-	if(match("char*", current_target->name))
-	{
-		assign = load_value(1, TRUE);
-	}
-	else
+	/* THE NAME COMPARISON, ACTUALLY REMOVED THIS TIME.
+	 *
+	 * The comment above says a name cannot answer a question about width, and
+	 * then this asked one anyway -- and it decided TWO things: a one-byte load
+	 * AND no stride multiply at all. Both are right for `char *p; p[i]`, where
+	 * the element is a char. Both are wrong for `char *arr[8]`, where the
+	 * element is a char POINTER: the index went unscaled and one signed byte
+	 * of an eight-byte pointer was loaded. `current_target` is `char *` in
+	 * BOTH cases, which is exactly why a name cannot separate them.
+	 *
+	 * element_size already separates them, six lines up, via
+	 * indexing_an_array. Using it means the load width and the stride agree by
+	 * construction rather than by coincidence -- which is what the comment
+	 * above claimed and the code below did not do.
+	 *
+	 * `assign` is left as the earlier branch set it, so an `unsigned char`
+	 * array keeps its unsigned load; the old override forced TRUE and would
+	 * have sign-extended it.
+	 *
+	 * FOUND BY BORROWING STAGE 2's CONFORMANCE CORPUS. Nine of its 426
+	 * programs fail on this -- 394, 396, 397, 398, 400, 401, 402, 403, 404 --
+	 * and the stage-3 case suite has been green over it every round, because
+	 * every array-of-pointers case in it used `long*`, where the element width
+	 * and the pointed-at width are both 8. */
+	if(1 != element_size)
 	{
 		emit_mul_register_zero_with_immediate(element_size, "primary expr array");
 	}
